alex-customer: Hey team — after upgrading our TS SDK from 0.9.x -> 1.2.0 our embeddings pipeline started failing. We get dimension mismatch errors from the API and intermittent timeouts when we try to batch. This used to work fine.

maria-support: Sorry you're hitting this, Alex. Can you paste the exact error and a minimal repro (TS/Python) so we can triage? Also which model are you calling?

alex-customer: Error shows up in our logs like this: `HTTP 400 Bad Request - EmbeddingDimensionMismatch: expected 1536, got 1024` — occurs when we send bulk requests (batching). If we send single inputs it succeeds but is slow. Using model `embed-v2-small` (we thought it matched old dims). We also see occasional `504 Gateway Timeout` when we do 64 docs per request.

samir-eng: Two quick possibilities: (1) model change — some newer embed models use different dims; (2) SDK batching code path might be splitting inputs into chunks and accidentally sending to a model with a different expected dim. Can you share the request payload (headers + body) for one failing batch?

alex-customer: Minimal TS snippet we use (simplified):

```ts
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference-js';
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

await client.embeddings.create({
  model: 'embed-v2-small',
  input: docs, // array of ~64 short strings
  batchSize: 64,
  timeoutMs: 30000,
});
```

When it fails the server returns the above 400. If I change to `batchSize: 1` all items succeed (but slow).

emily-sdk: Thanks — a couple notes from SDK side: we shipped a change in 1.2.0 where `batchSize` param was renamed to `maxBatchSize` internally for some call paths (TS bug #2147). If you passed `batchSize` it might be ignored in certain code paths and default batching/concurrency could route batches differently. But that shouldn't change embedding dims — that implies a mismatch between model configured on client and model actually served.

maria-support: Can you share raw server response for a failing batch (body)? Example curl we run for debugging: `curl -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/embeddings -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"model":"embed-v2-small", "input":["a","b","c"]}'` — what do you get?

alex-customer: Here's a sanitized failing response: 

```
HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request
{
  "error": {
    "type": "invalid_request_error",
    "message": "EmbeddingDimensionMismatch: model 'embed-v2-small' expects dimension 1536 but request yielded 1024",
    "param": null
  }
}
```

Also saw this in one of the node logs: `WARN sdk: routing to fallback model embed-v1-legacy due to capacity`, then the mismatch occurs. So looks like routing fallback mixed models.

samir-eng: That log is key — if the platform automatically falls back to a capacity-compatible model variant (embed-v1-legacy) which has a different dimension, and the SDK still thinks it's working with embed-v2-small, clients that assume a fixed dim will break. We have two bugs to investigate: the routing fallback not advertising the variant to the client, and the SDK's batch path ignoring the batchSize rename.

emily-sdk: On the SDK we also noticed a bug where the TypeScript client reuses a single request object when splitting batches, and a header `x-embed-model` could be overwritten in async interleaving. That could cause some sub-batches to hit a different model route. Patch in progress. Workaround for now: set `maxConcurrency: 1` and explicit `maxBatchSize` in options, or call the embeddings endpoint with single-item calls while we patch. Example patched call: `client.embeddings.create({model: 'embed-v2-small', input: docs, maxBatchSize: 64, maxConcurrency: 1, timeoutMs: 60000})`

alex-customer: Ok trying now. Also, is there a way to detect in response the dimension we actually got back so we can fail fast instead of silently indexing wrong vectors?

maria-support: Good question. Current API does not return model-dimension metadata in each response to keep payload small. Two immediate options: (1) call the `GET /v1/models/embed-v2-small` metadata endpoint once at startup to confirm `embedding_dimensions` and assert before indexing; (2) post-process and validate embedding length on client before writing to DB and raise an alert. We'll file a small ER to include dims in future responses.

eng-oncall-bot: Incident created: INC-2026-1847 — routing fallback + TS SDK batch concurrency causing mixed-model embeddings. Assigned: samir-eng, emily-sdk. Priority: P2.

samir-eng: Repro steps I'm using locally: spin up a load test that sends 1000 small documents with `maxBatchSize` = 64 and `maxConcurrency` = 8. Force capacity pressure by creating a rate limit header `x-force-capacity=low` to trigger fallback. Observed about ~8% of batches routed to embed-v1-legacy (dim 1024) while the client kept using 1536 assumption. Logs: `router: fallback_to_variant embed-v1-legacy reason=cap_pressure session=...`

emily-sdk: Quick SDK mitigation published as `1.2.1-beta` — it fixes the `batchSize` rename, ensures batch creation clones the request object per sub-batch, and respects `maxConcurrency`. Please test with this beta and confirm. Install: `npm i @redwood/inference-js@1.2.1-beta.3`

alex-customer: Installed beta and ran a small job with `maxConcurrency:1` and `maxBatchSize:64` — so far no dimension mismatches and latency improved vs single calls. I'm still seeing a couple of `504` when concurrency > 4.

samir-eng: The 504s likely from backend queue timeouts under burst. Another mitigation: enable client-side retry/backoff for 5xx/502/504 with jitter. Example simple retry policy in JS: 

```ts
async function retry(fn, retries = 3) {
  for (let i = 0; i < retries; i++) {
    try { return await fn(); } catch (e) {
      if (i === retries -1) throw e;
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, Math.pow(2, i) * 200 + Math.random()*50));
    }
  }
}

await retry(() => client.embeddings.create({model: 'embed-v2-small', input: batch}));
```

maria-support: Also remember to validate embedding length before indexing into your vector DB. If dims mismatch, drop and log with full request_id and batch index. We'll add a server-side metric to count dim-mismatches to alert us if fallback happens more than expected.

alex-customer: Thanks — the beta fix + reduced concurrency + retries looks promising. Please keep me posted on the stable release. Can you also confirm there won't be billing discrepancies if some batches used fallback models? We bill per-embedding token but dims affect downstream storage costs.

maria-support: I'll escalate billing concern to finance ops — if fallback models materially change costs we'll provide an adjustment path for affected customers. For now we will mark this as part of the incident and include a follow-up with any credits if appropriate.

samir-eng: Root cause summary: router fallback + TS SDK batching concurrency bug resulted in mixed-model requests; outcome was dimension mismatch on client-side and increased 5xx under high concurrency. Fixes: router now tags responses with `x-embed-variant` header (hotfix deployed), SDK patched in 1.2.1 stable (pr merged), and we'll add server metric + model-dim metadata API call recommended as part of SDK README.

alex-customer: Great, thanks team. I'll validate at scale next week and update.

emily-sdk: Closing loop — 1.2.1 is out in stable channel now. If you still see issues after upgrading, please reopen this thread and ping @maria-support and @samir-eng. :thumbsup:

maria-support: Marking as resolved for now, tracking follow-ups in INC-2026-1847 and SDK issue #2147.
